MEASUREMENT METHOD (unchanged from steps 1 / 1b / 1c)
  docker compose logs -t --no-log-prefix <node>  ->  EVERY line matching TELEMETRY
  -> all docker CAPTURE timestamps -> sorted -> adjacent pairs differenced.
  No --since. No first-line-of-a-kind. No de-duplication by content. A gap is
  counted only when BOTH endpoints fall inside the cut window, so a cut can
  neither manufacture a gap nor hide one. Percentiles are nearest-rank, the same
  rule in every table. Timestamps are docker's capture stamps, not stamps inside
  the telemetry line.

  Conditions A/C/B ran on ONE net with NO restarts between them — the budget was
  changed live with `docker update`, so build, tip range, difficulty regime and
  process lifetime are shared across those three and cannot explain a difference
  between them. Condition D is a separate FRESH net (a limit cannot be removed
  from a running container; see the docker-update note below), so D differs from
  A/C/B in tip range and process age as well as in budget.

THE FOUR CONDITIONS
  A  quota 2.00 CPU, dedicated core PAIR per node (0-1/2-3/4-5/6-7), nproc=2
     ... the condition the dispatch asked for: the hosts' 2 vCPU
  C  quota 0.40 CPU, ONE dedicated core per node, nproc=1
     ... a budget BELOW measured demand. Positive control, NOT a host model.
  B  quota raised to 18.00 CPU, cpuset 0-17, nproc=18   (same net as A and C)
     ... within-net unconstrained control
  D  compose DEFAULTS: NanoCpus=0, CpusetCpus="", cgroup quota=max, nproc=18
     ... proof that the default path is genuinely limit-free, plus a second
         unconstrained control on a fresh net

WHY nr_throttled IS THE NUMBER THAT DECIDES THIS RUN
  A CPU limit that is never reached is installed, verified, and inert. cgroup v2's
  cpu.stat reports how many 100 ms enforcement periods the cgroup was actually cut
  off in. In condition A that count is ZERO on every node across ~19,000 periods:
  the budget the dispatch asked for was correctly applied and was never the binding
  constraint. So A's negative is a statement about the WORKLOAD'S DEMAND, not about
  CPU scarcity — which is why condition C exists.

A TRAP, FOUND BY TESTING RATHER THAN BY READING
  `docker update --cpus 0 --cpuset-cpus ""` exits 0 and prints the container name
  and CHANGES NOTHING — docker reads 0/"" as "leave this field alone", not as
  "remove the limit". Verified directly:
      BEFORE cpu.max=200000 100000 cpuset=0-1 nproc=2
      (docker update --cpus 0 --cpuset-cpus "" ; exit 0, prints name)
      AFTER  cpu.max=200000 100000 cpuset=0-1 nproc=2      <-- unchanged
  Raising works (--cpus 18 --cpuset-cpus 0-17 -> cpu.max=1800000 100000, nproc=18).
  So `soak.sh cpu-budget none` RAISES the ceiling and says so, and only a fresh
  `up` at the compose defaults gives NanoCpus=0 (condition D).

