  A  quota 2.00  03:24:1x -> 03:59:48   56.07 CPU-s over ~2130 s wall = 2.6% of ONE core
  C  quota 0.40  03:59:59 -> 04:19:51   27.37 CPU-s over  1192 s wall = 2.3% of ONE core
  B  quota 18.0  04:20:40 -> 04:35:21   40.84 CPU-s over   881 s wall = 4.6% of ONE core
  D  no quota    04:43:5x -> 04:59:44   27.30 CPU-s over  ~950 s wall = 2.9% of ONE core
  (node0 figures; the other three are within ~10% on every line — see cpu-show dumps.)

  docker stats, 60 s samples, all four nodes pooled per condition:
    A quota 2.00   n=140  mean 1.32%  min 0.45%  max  27.44%   (% of the container's own cores)
    C quota 0.40   n= 76  mean 2.08%  min 0.28%  max  40.55%
    B quota 18.0   n= 56  mean 9.42%  min 0.29%  max 100.44%
    D no quota     n= 60  mean 1.84%  min 0.42%  max  81.73%
  The maxima matter more than the means: a mine attempt bursts to ~ONE core. Average
  demand is ~3% of a core; INSTANTANEOUS demand during mining is ~100% of one core.
  That is why 0.40 CPU throttles and 2.00 CPU cannot: CFS decides throttling inside
  each 100 ms period, and a single-threaded burst wanting one core is cut off by any
  quota below 1.00 CPU and by no quota at or above it.

